          "fact_ids": [
            "age_sex.0",
            "presenting_complaint.0",
            "diagnosis_primary.0"
          ],
          "text": "This 28-year-old male was admitted with an 18-hour history of right iliac fossa pain, nausea, vomiting and anorexia, and was diagnosed with acute appendicitis.",
          "unverified_fact_ids": []
        },
        {
          "section_id": "sections.12",
          "part": "part_b",
          "heading": "admission_summary",
          "fact_ids": [
            "key_investigations.0",
            "key_investigations.1",
            "key_investigations.2",
            "key_investigations.3",
            "treatment.0",
            "treatment.1",
            "treatment.2",
            "diagnosis_background.0"
          ],
          "text": "He has no past medical history. Investigations showed a white cell count of 14.2 and CRP 68; urine dipstick was negative; ultrasound demonstrated free fluid in the right iliac fossa with the appendix not well visualised. He was managed with nil by mouth, IV fluids and IV co-amoxiclav, and proceeded to laparoscopic appendicectomy. Intraoperative findings confirmed an acutely inflamed appendix with no perforation; the procedure was uncomplicated with minimal blood loss. Absorbable sutures were used and do not require removal. Histology is pending.",
